}, {"contents": [{"content": "Quiz", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}], "id": 10, "type": "RSLT"}, {"questions": [{"responses": [{"_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "selected": 0, "id": 0, "correct": 1}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "enpe7n2le02ob05re7pdf01.pdf", "type": "pdf"}], "type": "question"}], "id": 0}], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "Now it's your turn to find more information the documentation of program code. ", "type": "text"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Take Away", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}], "type": "post_instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}], "type": "learning_skills"}], "id": 11, "type": "DWS"}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}], "type": "lab_options"}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "audio", "title": "", "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n2le02ob05re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "0", "text": "<strong>Alex:</strong> What are you doing?\n</br><strong>Paul:</strong> I am reading an article about documentation of program code. It states that documentation code refers to the program listings or technical manuals which describe the operation and use of programs. It illustrates and explains how a software operates or how to use it.</br><strong>Alex:</strong> I don't know anything about it. Tell me something else. I can't imagine doing it by myself. I guess it isn't enough information for me. </br>", "image": "enpe7n2le02ob05re2img01.jpg", "id": 0}, {"time": "30500", "text": "<strong>Paul:</strong> Actually, it is not. Let me explain a little bit more what the program code is. It can be divided into two main categories. It doesn't refer only to manuals, but also to training materials for someone who focuses on how to use the program's features to meet somebody's needs. It also focuses on documentation for programmers involved in software development and maintenance.\n</br><strong>Alex:</strong> I heard that many years ago, programmers used punch cards.\n</br><strong>Paul:</strong> Right!  Programmers used comments for describing the operation of a section of code. With punched cards, programmers had minimal facilities for incorporating comments. Documenting code and adding comments became easier when programmers switched from punch card input to the use of keyboards. </br>", "image": "enpe7n2le02ob05re2img02.jpg", "id": 1}, {"time": "86300", "text": "<strong>Alex:</strong> It sounds something similar to what programmers do with JAVA and C+.  Is there anything that a person who is not a software developer can understand?\n</br><strong>Paul:</strong> Yes, there is something called external documentation. It shows design documents; they can be simple flowcharts, outlines or detailed information of the program's purpose, structure, and operations. Documentation is an important part of software engineering. It includes architecture, design, overview of software; also, the technical aspect considers documentation of code, algorithms, and interfaces. Finally, it considers the user. They create manuals for everyone, end users, system administrators and support staff.\n</br><strong>Alex:</strong> Thank you for explaining all this; it was very interesting.</br>", "image": "enpe7n2le02ob05re2img03.jpg", "id": 2}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n2le02ob05re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}]}